WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.064
Harvest-now, decrypt-later planning requires a basic understanding of cryptographic roles.

2
00:00:06.064 --> 00:00:13.326
Public-key cryptography helps systems establish trust or shared secrets without already having a shared secret.

3
00:00:13.326 --> 00:00:22.309
It appears in certificates, key exchange, digital signatures, TLS handshakes, VPNs, SSH, secure email, code signing, and identity systems.

4
00:00:22.309 --> 00:00:34.812
Some widely used public-key algorithms are the main focus of quantum-related migration planning because their security assumptions may not hold against sufficiently capable quantum computers.

5
00:00:34.812 --> 00:00:37.582
Symmetric encryption has a different role.

6
00:00:37.582 --> 00:00:43.496
Once two parties share a secret key, symmetric algorithms can protect bulk data efficiently.

7
00:00:43.496 --> 00:00:53.303
In TLS, for example, public-key mechanisms help set up the protected session, while symmetric encryption protects application data after the handshake.

8
00:00:53.303 --> 00:00:57.346
Hashing and message authentication also have important roles.

9
00:00:57.346 --> 00:01:07.153
These functions are affected differently by quantum considerations, so teams should avoid treating all cryptography as one undifferentiated category.

10
00:01:07.153 --> 00:01:11.196
Key exchange and digital signatures solve different problems.

11
00:01:11.196 --> 00:01:16.286
Key exchange or key establishment helps parties derive shared secret material.

12
00:01:16.286 --> 00:01:22.350
Digital signatures help verify the identity of a signer and detect modification of signed data.

13
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.495
Certificates connect public keys to identities.

14
00:01:25.495 --> 00:01:28.639
In a TLS or VPN setting, these roles work together.

15
00:01:28.639 --> 00:01:39.345
If the public-key portion becomes breakable later, captured encrypted sessions may become more vulnerable depending on protocol details and implementation choices.

16
00:01:39.345 --> 00:01:42.863
The planning message is not that encryption is useless.

17
00:01:42.863 --> 00:01:46.606
Strong, well-implemented encryption is still essential.

18
00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:56.414
The message is that long-term confidentiality depends on the assumptions behind the cryptographic system, not just on the presence of encryption labels.

19
00:01:56.414 --> 00:02:08.317
Teams should identify which algorithms, protocols, libraries, keys, and certificates protect long-lived data, then plan migration using standards-based and well-tested mechanisms.
